```python
def contains(s, sa, pattern):
    lo, hi = 0, len(sa) - 1
    m = len(pattern)
    while lo <= hi:
        mid = (lo + hi) // 2
        suffix = s[sa[mid]: sa[mid] + m]
        if suffix == pattern:
            return True
        elif suffix < pattern:
            lo = mid + 1
        else:
            hi = mid - 1
    return False
```

Because the suffix array is sorted, checking whether `pattern` occurs anywhere in `s` is a standard binary search over it — O(log n) suffix comparisons, each costing up to O(m) to compare the pattern's length, giving **O(m log n)** total. That beats a linear scan of the text for repeated queries against the same string, since the O(n log n) suffix-array build is a one-time cost amortized across many searches.
